t<tr id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n757\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">out-of-domain</td>\n758\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#99D5A9;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.2.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#99D5A9;\">3.28</span></td>\n759\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#C2E6CB;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.3.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#C2E6CB;\">16.27</span></td>\n760\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#CAE9D2;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.4.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#CAE9D2;\">0.7148</span></td>\n761\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EE;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.5.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EE;\">0.5928</span></td>\n762\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.6\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E0F2E5;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.6.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E0F2E5;\">0.5820</span></td>\n763\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.7\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E8F5EB;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.12.12.7.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E8F5EB;\">0.5489</span></td>\n764\t</tr>\n765\t<tr id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n766\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\">F-CosyVoice-idx</td>\n767\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\">in-domain</td>\n768\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#9CD6AC;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.3.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#9CD6AC;\">3.25</span></td>\n769\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#96D4A6;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.4.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#96D4A6;\">11.92</span></td>\n770\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#D2ECD9;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.5.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#D2ECD9;\">0.7043</span></td>\n771\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.6\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#CBEAD3;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.6.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#CBEAD3;\">0.7383</span></td>\n772\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.7\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E0F2E5;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.7.1\" class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#E0F2E5;\">0.5811</span></td>\n773\t<td id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.8\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#D3EDDA;\"><span id=\"S4.T5.1.13.13.8.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\" style=\"--ltx-bg-color:#D3EDDA;\">0.6565</span></td>\n774\t</tr>\n775\t</tbody>\n776\t</table>\n777\t</figure>\n778\t<figure id=\"S4.F5\" class=\"ltx_figure\">\n779\t<p id=\"S4.F5.1\" class=\"ltx_p ltx_align_center\"><span id=\"S4.F5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\"><img src=\"2607.23027v1/x6.png\" id=\"S4.F5.1.1.g1\" class=\"ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape\" width=\"458\" height=\"166\" alt=\"Refer to caption\"></span></p>\n780\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure\">Figure 5: </span>In-/out-of-domain distribution across systems.</figcaption>\n781\t</figure>\n782\t</section>\n783\t<section id=\"S4.SS5\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n784\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n785\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S4.SS5.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">IV-E</span> </span><span id=\"S4.SS5.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Inference Chatterbox</span>\n786\t</h3>\n787\t\n788\t<div id=\"S4.SS5.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n789\t<p id=\"S4.SS5.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">During the inference phase in Fig. <a href=\"#S1.F2\" title=\"Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a>, speech is synthesized by two Chatterbox systems: the off-the-shelf zero-shot baseline, <span id=\"S4.SS5.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Chatterbox</span>. and the Singlish fine-tuned model, <span id=\"S4.SS5.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-Chatterbox</span>, in Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T5\" title=\"TABLE V ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">V</span></a>. These two systems share an identical pipeline and decoding configurations and differ only in the T3 <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Chatterbox-Flash: Prior-Calibrated Block Diffusion for Streaming Zero-Shot TTS\" class=\"ltx_ref\">34</a>]</cite> module weights, <span id=\"S4.SS5.p1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-Chatterbox</span> uses fine-tuned T3. While S3Gen flow-matching and the VoiceEncoder modules remain the original pretrained modules in both. Holding the pipeline fixed makes any difference attributable to adaptation, which we probe along the in-domain vs. out-of-domain axis. This separates how the model renders the accent for familiar voices (adaptation) from how it generalizes to new ones (consistency).</p>\n790\t</div>\n791\t<div id=\"S4.SS5.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n792\t<p id=\"S4.SS5.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For each speaker from the <span id=\"S4.SS5.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">in-domain</span> evaluation split, all transcripts are synthesized using a single reference recording (audio prompt, <math id=\"S4.SS5.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sim\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>8 seconds), the audio prompt is selected per-speaker and reused across all of that speaker’s utterances. The same audio prompts were used for the off-the-shelf <span id=\"S4.SS5.p2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Chatterbox</span> and <span id=\"S4.SS5.p2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-Chatterbox</span> to keep the set up fixed.\n793\tThe synthesizer hyper parameters, Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T6\" title=\"TABLE VI ‣ IV-E Inference Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">VI</span></a>, were selected on the in-domain validation split.</p>\n794\t</div>\n795\t<div id=\"S4.SS5.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n796\t<p id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Comparing the off-the-shelf <span id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Chatterbox</span> against <span id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-Chatterbox</span>, the off-the-shelf is run zero-shot through the identical pipeline. Here every speaker is unseen, and the speaker characteristics are extracted from the same speaker audio prompts. The synthesizer is thus exercised over <span id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">system</span> <math id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\times\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\times</annotation></semantics></math> <span id=\"S4.SS5.p3.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">domain</span>, and each synthesized set is assessed under the objective metrics in Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T5\" title=\"TABLE V ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">V</span></a>.</p>\n797\t</div>\n798\t<figure id=\"S4.T6\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n799\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\">TABLE VI: </span>Chatterbox and F-Chatterbox inference hyperparameters</figcaption>\n800\t<table id=\"S4.T6.1\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle\">\n801\t<thead class=\"ltx_thead\">\n802\t<tr id=\"S4.T6.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n803\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Parameter</span></th>\n804\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.1.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Value</span></th>\n805\t</tr>\n806\t</thead>\n807\t<tbody class=\"ltx_tbody\">\n808\t<tr id=\"S4.T6.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n809\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.2.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">temperature</span></th>\n810\t<td id=\"S4.T6.1.2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\">0.75</td>\n811\t</tr>\n812\t<tr id=\"S4.T6.1.3.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n813\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.3.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.3.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">cfg_weight</span></th>\n814\t<td id=\"S4.T6.1.3.2.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">0.50</td>\n815\t</tr>\n816\t<tr id=\"S4.T6.1.4.3\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n817\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.4.3.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.4.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">repetition_penalty</span></th>\n818\t<td id=\"S4.T6.1.4.3.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">1.35</td>\n819\t</tr>\n820\t<tr id=\"S4.T6.1.5.4\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n821\t<th id=\"S4.T6.1.5.4.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb\"><span id=\"S4.T6.1.5.4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">exaggeration</span></th>\n822\t<td id=\"S4.T6.1.5.4.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">0.40</td>\n823\t</tr>\n824\t</tbody>\n825\t</table>\n826\t</figure>\n827\t</section>\n828\t<section id=\"S4.SS6\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n829\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n830\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S4.SS6.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">IV-F</span> </span><span id=\"S4.SS6.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Evaluation Metrics</span>\n831\t</h3>\n832\t\n833\t<div id=\"S4.SS6.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n834\t<p id=\"S4.SS6.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We evaluate real and generated datasets by all systems with the objective metrics <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"CodecMOS-Accent: a MOS benchmark of resynthesized and TTS speech from neural codecs across English accents\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite> for Singlish accent capability benchmarking. We conduct objective evaluation using the following four commonly used metrics. Each similarity metric is reported in two forms. The <span id=\"S4.SS6.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">prompt</span> variant is the cosine similarity between the synthesized utterance and the single-shot audio prompt (voice cloning reference) that conditioned generation. It measures how faithfully the system reproduces the specific reference voice. The <span id=\"S4.SS6.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span> variant is the cosine similarity of generated against the target ground-truth recording. Where generated and the real utterance having the same-text content. It measures generalization rather than memorized prompt content. The Ground Truth scores exactly 1 on both <span id=\"S4.SS6.p1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span> columns in Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T5\" title=\"TABLE V ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">V</span></a>, since a real utterance is compared to itself and <math id=\"S4.SS6.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"&lt;1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> on the prompt columns (cross-content, same-speaker ceiling).</p>\n835\t</div>\n836\t<div id=\"S4.SS6.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n837\t<p id=\"S4.SS6.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.SS6.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic\">Naturalness (UT-MOS)</span>. We report naturalness with the UT-MOS <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib42\" title=\"UTMOS: utokyo-sarulab system for voicemos challenge 2022\" class=\"ltx_ref\">33</a>]</cite> neural MOS predictor, an SSL-ensemble system from the VoiceMOS Challenge line of work <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib43\" title=\"The voicemos challenge 2023: zero-shot subjective speech quality prediction for multiple domains\" class=\"ltx_ref\">5</a>]</cite>. We treat UT-MOS cautiously: the predictor is trained predominantly on clean, read studio speech and systematically reward clean synthetic audio over spontaneous accented recordings. UT-MOS is therefore read as a sanity check and a diagnostic of distance from clean-synthetic norms.</p>\n838\t</div>\n839\t<div id=\"S4.SS6.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n840\t<p id=\"S4.SS6.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.SS6.p3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic\">Intelligibility (WER)</span>. Word error rate is the standard intelligibility proxy across zero-shot TTS evaluation <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib23\" title=\"TTSDS - Text-to-Speech Distribution Score\" class=\"ltx_ref\">27</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"CodecMOS-Accent: a MOS benchmark of resynthesized and TTS speech from neural codecs across English accents\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>. It measures whether generated Singlish speech remains decodable. We transcribe with the Singlish Whisper ASR model <span id=\"footnote3\" class=\"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">3</sup><span class=\"ltx_note_outer\"><span class=\"ltx_note_content\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">3</sup><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note\">3</span><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/mjwong/whisper-large-v3-turbo-singlish\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref ltx_href\">https://huggingface.co/mjwong/whisper-large-v3-turbo-singlish</a></span></span></span>.</p>\n841\t</div>\n842\t<div id=\"S4.SS6.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n843\t<p id=\"S4.SS6.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.SS6.p4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic\">Speaker similarity (SPK-SIM)</span>. It is the cosine similarity between ECAPA-TDNN speaker embeddings <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib21\" title=\"ECAPA-TDNN: Emphasized Channel Attention, Propagation and Aggregation in TDNN Based Speaker Verification\" class=\"ltx_ref\">6</a>]</cite> of the synthesized and reference utterance. Where the reference is fixed reference audio prompt or all utterances of the ground truth dataset (prompt/match modes).</p>\n844\t</div>\n845\t<div id=\"S4.SS6.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n846\t<p id=\"S4.SS6.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.SS6.p5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic\">Accent similarity (ACC-SIM)</span>. Our headline metric is the cosine similarity in the accent-discriminative embedding space, computing similarity from the CommonAccent ECAPA-TDNN model<span id=\"footnote4\" class=\"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">4</sup><span class=\"ltx_note_outer\"><span class=\"ltx_note_content\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">4</sup><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note\">4</span><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/Jzuluaga/accent-id-commonaccent_ecapa\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref ltx_href\">https://huggingface.co/Jzuluaga/accent-id-commonaccent_ecapa</a></span></span></span> <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"CommonAccent: Exploring Large Acoustic Pretrained Models for Accent Classification Based on Common Voice\" class=\"ltx_ref\">41</a>]</cite>, it is pretrained for English-accent classification, including Singlish <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"CommonAccent: Exploring Large Acoustic Pretrained Models for Accent Classification Based on Common Voice\" class=\"ltx_ref\">41</a>]</cite>. Since the encoder reaches roughly <math id=\"S4.SS6.p5.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sim\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>90% accent-classification accuracy and clusters by phonological similarity, cosine distance in its space functions as an accent-fidelity score. The central question of the paper: how closely does generated speech reproduce the Singlish accent? Thus, the choice of ACC-SIM is motivated by <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"CodecMOS-Accent: a MOS benchmark of resynthesized and TTS speech from neural codecs across English accents\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>, where the analogous O-ACC-SIM was shown to track subjective accent-similarity judgments at scale <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"CodecMOS-Accent: a MOS benchmark of resynthesized and TTS speech from neural codecs across English accents\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>.</p>\n847\t</div>\n848\t</section>\n849\t</section>\n850\t<section id=\"S5\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n851\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n852\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">V </span><span id=\"S5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Results</span>\n853\t</h2>\n854\t\n855\t<div id=\"S5.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n856\t<p id=\"S5.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Table <a href=\"#S4.T5\" title=\"TABLE V ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">V</span></a> reports all four metrics for real speech (Ground Truth), the off-the-shelf base models, and their fine-tuned counterparts, on seen speakers during fine-tuning (in-domain) and unseen speakers (out-of-domain). Similarity is measured against the inference <span id=\"S5.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">prompt</span> and against the held-out target (<span id=\"S5.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span>); Ground Truth attains <span id=\"S5.p1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span> equal 1 by construction, and its <span id=\"S5.p1.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">prompt</span> values give a same-speaker, cross-content ceiling.</p>\n857\t</div>\n858\t<div id=\"S5.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n859\t<p id=\"S5.p2.17\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S5.p2.17.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Accent and Speaker Similarity.</span> ACC-SIM (<span id=\"S5.p2.17.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span>) is our\n860\tprimary indicator of accent transfer. Off-the-shelf, Chatterbox is weakest\n861\t(<math id=\"S5.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5114\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5114</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5114</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p2.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.4697\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.4697</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4697</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that single-shot prompting does not induce a\n862\tSinglish accent; fine-tuning yields the largest gain (<math id=\"S5.p2.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"+0.126\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.126</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.126</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p2.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"+0.092\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.092</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.092</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id=\"S5.p2.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6376\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6376</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6376</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p2.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5617\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5617</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5617</annotation></semantics></math>). CosyVoice starts higher (<math id=\"S5.p2.7.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5771\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5771</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5771</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p2.8.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5212\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5212</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5212</annotation></semantics></math>) and gains less (<math id=\"S5.p2.9.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"+0.027\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.027</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.027</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p2.10.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"+0.028\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.028</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.028</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with\n863\tbroader base-model coverage and less headroom. Speaker similarity follows the same pattern (Chatterbox <math id=\"S5.p2.11.m11\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6036\\!\\rightarrow\\!0.6949\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6036</mn><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0.6949</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6036\\!\\rightarrow\\!0.6949</annotation></semantics></math> in-domain; CosyVoice nearly flat). On the <span id=\"S5.p2.17.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">prompt</span> ACC-SIM metric F-Chatterbox even exceeds the Ground Truth ceiling (<math id=\"S5.p2.12.m12\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6748\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6748</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6748</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id=\"S5.p2.13.m13\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6277\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6277</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6277</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas\n864\tF-CosyVoice is <em id=\"S5.p2.17.4\" class=\"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic\">flat</em> (<math id=\"S5.p2.14.m14\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6200\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6200</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6200</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id=\"S5.p2.15.m15\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6277\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6277</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6277</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S5.p2.16.m16\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5820\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5820</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5820</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id=\"S5.p2.17.m17\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5802\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5802</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5802</annotation></semantics></math>) while its <span id=\"S5.p2.17.5\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span> ACC-SIM holds a drift toward a generalized Singlish voice rather than the specific prompt speaker.</p>\n865\t</div>\n866\t<div id=\"S5.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n867\t<p id=\"S5.p3.10\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S5.p3.10.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Intelligibility and Naturalness.</span> The base models fail on WER in\n868\topposite directions. Off-the-shelf Chatterbox is <em id=\"S5.p3.10.2\" class=\"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic\">more</em> intelligible\n869\tthan real speech (<math id=\"S5.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"11.48\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.48\\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id=\"S5.p3.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"17.31\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">17.31\\%</annotation></semantics></math>) by producing clean, accent-neutral\n870\toutput; fine-tuning raises its WER toward the real band (<math id=\"S5.p3.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"15.39\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15.39</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15.39\\%</annotation></semantics></math>), not away\n871\tfrom quality. CosyVoice instead mis-renders content from the prompt (<math id=\"S5.p3.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"19.20\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">19.20\\%</annotation></semantics></math>). We found CosyVoice is unstable on short utterances and hallucinating, which leads to inflated WER. Fine-tuning <em id=\"S5.p3.10.3\" class=\"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic\">lowers</em> its WER to <math id=\"S5.p3.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"14.68\\%\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.68\\%</annotation></semantics></math>. UT-MOS is reported with caution: real Singlish scores <em id=\"S5.p3.10.4\" class=\"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic\">lowest</em> (<math id=\"S5.p3.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"2.50\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>2.50</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.50</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id=\"S5.p3.7.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"2.37\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>2.37</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.37</annotation></semantics></math>)\n872\tand the off-the-shelf models highest (<math id=\"S5.p3.8.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"3.33\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>3.33</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.33</annotation></semantics></math> - <math id=\"S5.p3.9.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"3.35\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>3.35</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.35</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting the\n873\tpredictor’s bias toward clean synthetic audio <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"CodecMOS-Accent: a MOS benchmark of resynthesized and TTS speech from neural codecs across English accents\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>.\n874\tFine-tuning moves both toward the real value (most for F-Chatterbox,\n875\t<math id=\"S5.p3.10.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"3.34\\!\\rightarrow\\!2.75\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.34</mn><mo lspace=\"0.108em\" rspace=\"0.108em\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>2.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.34\\!\\rightarrow\\!2.75</annotation></semantics></math>), so lower UT-MOS here indicates closer to real condition speech,\n876\tnot degradation.</p>\n877\t</div>\n878\t<div id=\"S5.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n879\t<p id=\"S5.p4.5\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S5.p4.5.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Adaptation and Consistency.</span> In-domain scores exceed out-of-domain throughout, Fig. <a href=\"#S4.F5\" title=\"Figure 5 ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">5</span></a>, but the fine-tuned models retain their advantage on unseen speakers - F-Chatterbox’s out-of-domain accent similarity (<math id=\"S5.p4.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5617\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5617</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5617</annotation></semantics></math>) still beats off-the-shelf in-domain (<math id=\"S5.p4.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.5114\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.5114</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5114</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, the adaptation generalises rather than memorises. Finally, F-CosyVoice-idx, which conditions on a learned per-speaker representation rather than the prompt, attains the best <span id=\"S5.p4.5.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">match</span> similarity (SPK-SIM <math id=\"S5.p4.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.7383\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.7383</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.7383</annotation></semantics></math>, ACC-SIM <math id=\"S5.p4.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.6565\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>0.6565</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6565</annotation></semantics></math>) and the\n880\tlowest fine-tuned WER (<math id=\"S5.p4.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"11.92\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>11.92</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11.92</annotation></semantics></math>) but the lowest <span id=\"S5.p4.5.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">prompt</span> similarity,\n881\ttaking the drift above to its limit and applying only to seen speakers.</p>\n882\t</div>\n883\t</section>\n884\t<section id=\"S6\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n885\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n886\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">VI </span><span id=\"S6.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps\">Conclusions</span>\n887\t</h2>\n888\t\n889\t<div id=\"S6.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n890\t<p id=\"S6.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We presented the first systematic study of Singlish-accented zero-shot TTS. Off-the-shelf Chatterbox and CosyVoice 3, prompted with genuine Singlish references, preserve speaker timbre but flatten the accent - confirmed by low accent similarity against matched real recordings. Fine-tuning on 50 IMDA NSC <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"Building the Singapore English national speech corpus\" class=\"ltx_ref\">22</a>]</cite> speakers substantially closes this gap: matched ACC-SIM rises by up to <math id=\"S6.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"+0.13\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.13</annotation></semantics></math> in-domain, and the gain persists on 42 unseen speakers, showing the models learn the accent rather than memorize voices. The two backbones fail and recover differently - Chatterbox trades accent-neutral over-intelligibility for authentic Singlish delivery, while CosyVoice’s fine-tuning also repairs its hallucination-driven WER and speaker-index conditioning (F-CosyVoice-idx) pushes accent fidelity furthest at the cost of prompt faithfulness and open-set use.</p>\n891\t</div>\n892\t</section>\n893\t<section id=\"Sx1\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n894\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_section\">AI-Generated Content Disclosure</h2>\n895\t\n896\t<div id=\"Sx1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n897\t<p id=\"Sx1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The authors used Anthropic’s Claude and OpenAI’s ChatGPT as writing and editing assistants in preparing this manuscript. The tools were used to draft and revise prose in the abstract, introduction, related work, results, and discussion sections, and to generate and refine the plotting code for the figures from numerical results produced by the authors. All research design, experiments, data, quantitative results, and scientific claims are the authors’ own; all AI-assisted text and figures were reviewed, verified, and edited by the authors, who take full responsibility for the content.</p>\n898\t</div>\n899\t</section>\n900\t<section id=\"bib\" class=\"ltx_bibliography\">\n901\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography\">References</h2>\n902\t\n903\t<ul id=\"bib.L1\" class=\"ltx_biblist\">\n904\t<li id=\"bib.bib39\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_inproceedings\">\n905\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[1]</span>\n906\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">E. Casanova, K. Davis, E. Gölge, G. Göknar, I. Gulea, L. Hart, A. Aljafari, J. Meyer, R. Morais, S. Olayemi, <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal\">et al.</span></span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2024)</span>\n907\t</span>\n908\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">XTTS: a massively multilingual zero-shot text-to-speech model</span>.\n909\t</span>\n910\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\">In <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_inbook\">Proc. Interspeech 2024</span>,\n911\t</span>\n912\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_pages\"> pp. 4978–4982</span>.\n913\t</span>\n914\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S2.p1.1\" title=\"II Related Work ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§II</span></a>.\n915\t</span>\n916\t</li>\n917\t<li id=\"bib.bib5\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_article\">\n918\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[2]</span>\n919\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">S. Chen, S. Liu, L. Zhou, Y. Liu, X. Tan, J. Li, S. Zhao, Y. Qian, and F. Wei</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2024)</span>\n920\t</span>\n921\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">VALL-E 2: neural codec language models are human parity zero-shot text to speech synthesizers.</span>.\n922\t</span>\n923\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_journal\">CoRR</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_volume\">abs/2406.05370</span>.\n924\t</span>\n925\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S1.p1.1\" title=\"I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§I</span></a>.\n926\t</span>\n927\t</li>\n928\t<li id=\"bib.bib4\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_article\">\n929\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[3]</span>\n930\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">S. Chen, C. Wang, Y. Wu, Z. Zhang, L. Zhou, S. Liu, Z. Chen, Y. Liu, H. Wang, J. Li, <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal\">et al.</span></span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2025)</span>\n931\t</span>\n932\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">Neural codec language models are zero-shot text to speech synthesizers</span>.\n933\t</span>\n934\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_journal\">IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech and Language Processing</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_volume\">33</span>, <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_pages\"> pp. 705–718</span>.\n935\t</span>\n936\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S1.p1.1\" title=\"I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§I</span></a>,\n937\t<a href=\"#S2.p1.1\" title=\"II Related Work ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§II</span></a>.\n938\t</span>\n939\t</li>\n940\t<li id=\"bib.bib9\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_inproceedings\">\n941\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[4]</span>\n942\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">Y. Chen, Z. Niu, Z. Ma, K. Deng, C. Wang, J. JianZhao, K. Yu, and X. Chen</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2025-07)</span>\n943\t</span>\n944\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">F5-TTS: a fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching</span>.\n945\t</span>\n946\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\">In <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_inbook\">Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)</span>,\n947\t</span>\n948\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_place\">Vienna, Austria</span>, <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_pages\"> pp. 6255–6271</span>.\n949\t</span>\n950\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S1.p1.1\" title=\"I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§I</span></a>,\n951\t<a href=\"#S2.p1.1\" title=\"II Related Work ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§II</span></a>.\n952\t</span>\n953\t</li>\n954\t<li id=\"bib.bib43\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_inproceedings\">\n955\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[5]</span>\n956\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">E. Cooper, W. Huang, Y. Tsao, H. Wang, T. Toda, and J. Yamagishi</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2023)</span>\n957\t</span>\n958\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">The voicemos challenge 2023: zero-shot subjective speech quality prediction for multiple domains</span>.\n959\t</span>\n960\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\">In <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_inbook\">2023 IEEE Automatic Speech Recognition and Understanding Workshop (ASRU)</span>,\n961\t</span>\n962\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_pages\"> pp. 1–7</span>.\n963\t</span>\n964\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S4.SS6.p2.1\" title=\"IV-F Evaluation Metrics ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§<span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-F</span></span></a>.\n965\t</span>\n966\t</li>\n967\t<li id=\"bib.bib21\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_inproceedings\">\n968\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[6]</span>\n969\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">B. Desplanques, J. Thienpondt, and K. Demuynck</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2020)</span>\n970\t</span>\n971\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">ECAPA-TDNN: Emphasized Channel Attention, Propagation and Aggregation in TDNN Based Speaker Verification</span>.\n972\t</span>\n973\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\">In <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_inbook\">Interspeech</span>,\n974\t</span>\n975\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_pages\"> pp. 3830–3834</span>.\n976\t</span>\n977\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S2.p3.1\" title=\"II Related Work ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§II</span></a>,\n978\t<a href=\"#S4.SS6.p4.1\" title=\"IV-F Evaluation Metrics ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§<span class=\"ltx_text\">IV-F</span></span></a>.\n979\t</span>\n980\t</li>\n981\t<li id=\"bib.bib13\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_book\">\n982\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[7]</span>\n983\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">D. Deterding</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2007)</span>\n984\t</span>\n985\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">Singapore English</span>.\n986\t</span>\n987\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_publisher\">Edinburgh University Press</span>.\n988\t</span>\n989\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited\">Cited by: <a href=\"#S1.p2.1\" title=\"I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§I</span></a>,\n990\t<a href=\"#S2.p2.1\" title=\"II Related Work ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">§II</span></a>.\n991\t</span>\n992\t</li>\n993\t<li id=\"bib.bib6\" class=\"ltx_bibitem ltx_bib_article\">\n994\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_bib_key ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem\">[8]</span>\n995\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_author\">Z. Du, Q. Chen, S. Zhang, K. Hu, H. Lu, Y. Yang, H. Hu, S. Zheng, Y. Gu, Z. Ma, Z. Gao, and Z. Yan</span><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_year\"> (2024)</span>\n996\t</span>\n997\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_title\">CosyVoice: a scalable multilingual zero-shot text-to-speech synthesizer based on supervised semantic tokens</span>.\n998\t</span>\n999\t<span class=\"ltx_bibblock\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_journal\">ArXiv</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_bib_volume\">abs/2407.05407</span>.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0875f29c-7907-471b-a803-4cb6c71a744a", "uuid": "a26c340d-6506-4d04-bacd-5f4a6614e1b9", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T10:25:01.773Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/singlish_html.html", "content": "<th id=\"S4.T2.2.2.15.13.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb\">LLM prompt</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T2.2.2.15.13.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">Speaker ID</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T2.2.2.15.13.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">Default</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section id=\"S4.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S4.SS3.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">IV-C</span> </span><span id=\"S4.SS3.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Inferencing CosyVoice 3</span>\n</h3>\n\n<div id=\"S4.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">During the inference phase in Fig. <a href=\"#S1.F2\" title=\"Figure 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a>, speech is synthesized by three CosyVoice 3 systems: the off-the-shelf zero-shot baseline, <span id=\"S4.SS3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">CosyVoice</span>, and the two Singlish fine-tuned variants, <span id=\"S4.SS3.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-CosyVoice-idx</span> and <span id=\"S4.SS3.p1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-CosyVoice</span>, in Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T5\" title=\"TABLE V ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">V</span></a>.</p>\n</div>\n<div id=\"S4.SS3.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS3.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.SS3.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">CosyVoice</span> and <span id=\"S4.SS3.p2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-CosyVoice</span> operate in zero-shot mode, which conditions speech generation on a prompt speech and its corresponding transcript. In contrast, <span id=\"S4.SS3.p2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-CosyVoice-idx</span> operates in speaker fine-tuned (SFT) mode, which conditions speech generation solely on a speaker ID without requiring prompt speech. Since speaker IDs are only learned for speakers seen during fine-tuning, <span id=\"S4.SS3.p2.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">F-CosyVoice-idx</span> is evaluated only on the in-domain set.</p>\n</div>\n<div id=\"S4.SS3.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS3.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">These three systems differ only in LLM and Flow Matching model weights, LLM prompt, and inference mode, as summarized in Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T3\" title=\"TABLE III ‣ IV-C Inferencing CosyVoice 3 ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">III</span></a>. All other decoding parameters are kept unchanged from the official CosyVoice 3 inference script <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib25\" title=\"CosyVoice\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>]</cite>.</p>\n</div>\n<figure id=\"S4.T3\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\">TABLE III: </span>CosyVoice, F-CosyVoice-idx and F-CosyVoice inference setup</figcaption>\n<table id=\"S4.T3.1\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle\">\n<thead class=\"ltx_thead\">\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\"><span id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Parameter</span></th>\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\"><span id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">CosyVoice</span></th>\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\"><span id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.3.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">F-CosyVoice-idx</span></th>\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\"><span id=\"S4.T3.1.1.1.4.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">F-CosyVoice</span></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody class=\"ltx_tbody\">\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\">LLM</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\">Pretrained</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\">FT</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.2.1.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\">FT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.1.3.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.3.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">Flow Matching</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.3.2.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">Pretrained</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.3.2.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">FT</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.3.2.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">FT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.1.4.3\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.4.3.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">LLM prompt</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.4.3.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">Default</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.4.3.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">Speaker ID</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.4.3.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\">Default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T3.1.5.4\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T3.1.5.4.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb\">Inference mode</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.5.4.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">Zero-shot</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.5.4.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">SFT</td>\n<td id=\"S4.T3.1.5.4.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb\">Zero-shot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section id=\"S4.SS4\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\"><span id=\"S4.SS4.5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">IV-D</span> </span><span id=\"S4.SS4.6.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Fine-tuning Chatterbox</span>\n</h3>\n\n<div id=\"S4.SS4.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n<p id=\"S4.SS4.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">We adapt Chatterbox <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"Chatterbox-TTS\" class=\"ltx_ref\">32</a>]</cite> TTS to Singlish by fine-tuning only its T3 <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Chatterbox-Flash: Prior-Calibrated Block Diffusion for Streaming Zero-Shot TTS\" class=\"ltx_ref\">34</a>]</cite> module - the autoregressive Llama-style <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Chatterbox-Flash: Prior-Calibrated Block Diffusion for Streaming Zero-Shot TTS\" class=\"ltx_ref\">34</a>]</cite> transformer that maps text to discrete speech tokens while keeping the flow-matching decoder and the voice encoder frozen. For each utterance the text tokens are precomputed, <math id=\"S4.SS4.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sim\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>25Hz S3 speech tokens, an utterance-level speaker embedding, and a 3 seconds reference-prompt token sequence. The text vocabulary is extended to 2454 tokens. T3 module is optimized by next-token prediction with a combined cross-entropy over text and speech tokens. Detailed optimization hyper parameters were used from Tab. <a href=\"#S4.T4\" title=\"TABLE IV ‣ IV-D Fine-tuning Chatterbox ‣ IV Experimental Setup ‣ Singlish, Can or Not? Fine-Tuning and Evaluating Zero-Shot TTS for Singapore English\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">IV</span></a>.\nThe training loss decreases from 6.0 to below <math id=\"S4.SS4.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"10^{-3}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> and the adequacy of the adapted model was confirmed by subjective listening test to a few samples synthesized from <span id=\"S4.SS4.p1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">in-domain</span> validation split.</p>\n</div>\n<figure id=\"S4.T4\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\">TABLE IV: </span>Chatterbox and F-Chatterbox Fine-Tuning Setup and Hyperparameters</figcaption>\n<table id=\"S4.T4.2.2\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle\">\n<thead class=\"ltx_thead\">\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt\"><span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Parameter</span></th>\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\"><span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.3.1.2.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Value</span></span>\n</span>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody class=\"ltx_tbody\">\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.4.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\">Tuned module</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.4.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top ltx_border_t\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.4.1.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.4.1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">T3, full FT; S3Gen and VoiceEncoder frozen</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.5.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.5.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">Vocab</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.5.2.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.5.2.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.5.2.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">Extended to 2,454</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.6.3\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.6.3.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">Loss</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.6.3.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.6.3.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.6.3.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">Text CE + Speech CE</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\"><span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter\">emotion_adv</span></th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.7.4.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">0.5 (emotion-exaggeration)</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\">Optimizer</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top ltx_border_t\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">AdamW, LR=<math id=\"S4.T4.1.1.1.1.1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"10^{-5}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, Linear Decay, no warmup, bf16</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.8.5\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.8.5.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">Batch Size</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.8.5.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.8.5.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.8.5.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">16</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.9.6\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.9.6.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\">Epochs</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.9.6.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.9.6.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.9.6.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\">100 epochs = 73,600 steps</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n<th id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb\">Loss curve</th>\n<td id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n<span id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\" style=\"width:286.2pt;\"><math id=\"S4.T4.2.2.2.1.1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_math_unparsed\" alttext=\"6.0\\to{\\sim}10^{-3}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0</mn><mo rspace=\"0em\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\">∼</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\to{\\sim}10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> (after 100 epochs)</span>\n</span>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n<figure id=\"S4.T5\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n<figcaption class=\"lt